# Insight 9 SDK User Guide (Windows)

## 1. Overview

The Insight 9 Windows SDK provides a native Windows implementation for capturing data from three UVC cameras (RGB, grayscale, depth) and two HID devices (IMU, VIO). It also exposes camera parameter controls through the UVC extension unit.

This SDK automatically discovers supported devices, starts capture threads for each stream, and delivers image, IMU, and VIO data to applications via callbacks. It supports runtime parameter changes, per-camera start/stop, and depth-to-RGB alignment helpers.

## 2. Features

- **Automatic UVC/HID discovery**: Detects matching devices on Windows.
- **Multi-threaded capture**: Each stream runs in its own thread.
- **Callback-based delivery**: Image, IMU, and VIO data are provided in real time.
- **Camera parameter control**: Exposure, gain, brightness, contrast, gamma, hue, saturation, sharpness, white balance, and more.
- **Per-camera control**: Start/stop/restart cameras individually.
- **Calibration support**: Read intrinsics/extrinsics and align depth to RGB.
- **Windows build support**: Uses Visual Studio/CMake, prebuilt FFmpeg, HIDAPI, and OpenCV.

## 3. Dependencies

- **OS**: Windows x64
- **Build tools**: CMake, Visual Studio 2022/2026 (x64)
- **Optional runtime libraries**:
  - OpenCV (for `example.exe` display and image processing)
  - FFmpeg prebuilt libraries in `ffmpeg-z16`
  - HIDAPI prebuilt binary in `hidapi-prebuilt`

### 3.1 OpenCV

The project expects OpenCV under `third_party/opencv/build/x64/vc16`. Run `download_opencv.bat` before building if you do not already have OpenCV installed.

### 3.2 FFmpeg and HIDAPI

The Windows SDK ships with prebuilt FFmpeg and HIDAPI libraries:
- `ffmpeg-z16/lib/*.lib`
- `ffmpeg-z16/bin/*.dll`
- `hidapi-prebuilt/lib/hidapi.lib`
- `hidapi-prebuilt/bin/hidapi.dll`

## 4. Building and Installation

### 4.1 Quick build

Open a Developer Command Prompt for Visual Studio and run:

```bat
cd Windows-SDK
build.bat
```

This will create a `build` directory, configure CMake for `x64`, build the project in Release mode, and copy required DLLs next to `build\Release\example.exe`.

### 4.2 Manual CMake build

```bat
cd Windows-SDK
mkdir build
cd build
cmake .. -G "Visual Studio 18 2026" -A x64 -DUSE_SYSTEM_FFMPEG=OFF -DUSE_SYSTEM_HIDAPI=OFF
cmake --build . --config Release
```

### 4.3 Output

- `build\Release\example.exe`
- `build\Release\example.pdb`
- `build\Release\opencv_world*.dll` (copied automatically if available)
- `build\Release\avcodec-*.dll`, `avformat-*.dll`, `avdevice-*.dll`, `avutil-*.dll`, `swscale-*.dll`, `swresample-*.dll`
- `build\Release\hidapi.dll`

## 5. API Reference

Include the SDK header in your application:

```c
#include "Insight9SDK.h"
```

### 5.1 Core data structures

```c
typedef struct {
    uint8_t cam_id;
    uint8_t resolution;
    uint8_t frame_rate;
    float exposure_time;
    float exposure_gain;
    uint8_t auto_exposure;
    float brightness;
    float contrast;
    float gamma_dark;
    float hue;
    float saturation;
    uint8_t sharpness;
    uint8_t auto_white_balance;
    float white_balance;
    uint8_t decimation;
    uint8_t hardware_model;
} camera_params;
```

```c
typedef enum {
    PixelFormat_Unknown,
    PixelFormat_MJPEG,
    PixelFormat_GREY,
    PixelFormat_Z16,
    PixelFormat_RGB8,
    PixelFormat_Y8I,
    PixelFormat_YUYV
} PixelFormat;

typedef struct {
    int width;
    int height;
    int fps;
    PixelFormat pixel_format;
} video_config_t;

typedef struct {
    video_config_t rgb_config;
    video_config_t gray_config;
    video_config_t depth_config;
} insight9_config_t;
```

Calibration structs:

```c
typedef struct { ... } camera_intrinsics;
typedef struct { ... } camera_extrinsics;
typedef struct { camera_intrinsics intrinsics; camera_extrinsics extrinsics; } camera_calib;
```

### 5.2 Callback types

```c
typedef void (*image_callback)(int cam_id, uint8_t *data, size_t size,
                               int width, int height, unsigned int format,
                               uint64_t timestamp, uint64_t right_timestamp,
                               void *userdata);

typedef void (*imu_callback)(float ax, float ay, float az,
                             float gx, float gy, float gz,
                             uint64_t timestamp, void *userdata);

typedef void (*vio_callback)(float px, float py, float pz,
                             float qx, float qy, float qz, float qw,
                             uint64_t timestamp, void *userdata);
```

### 5.3 Initialization and control

```c
int insight9_receive_init_default(void);
int insight9_receive_init(const insight9_config_t* config);
int insight9_receive_start(void);
int insight9_receive_stop(void);
void insight9_receive_cleanup(void);
```

- `insight9_receive_init_default()` initializes the SDK with the built-in default stream configuration.
- `insight9_receive_init(&config)` initializes with a custom `insight9_config_t`.
- `insight9_receive_start()` begins capture for all cameras.
- `insight9_receive_stop()` stops all capture threads.
- `insight9_receive_cleanup()` frees resources.

### 5.4 Per-camera control

```c
int insight9_receive_start_camera(int cam_id);
void insight9_receive_stop_camera(int cam_id);
int insight9_receive_restart_camera(int cam_id);
int insight9_receive_is_camera_running(int cam_id);
```

### 5.5 Callbacks

```c
void insight9_receive_register_image_callback(image_callback cb, void *userdata);
void insight9_receive_register_imu_callback(imu_callback cb, void *userdata);
void insight9_receive_register_vio_callback(vio_callback cb, void *userdata);
```

Register callbacks before calling `insight9_receive_start()`.

### 5.6 Utility functions

```c
const char *insight9_receive_get_video_dev(int cam_id);
int insight9_receive_get_current_fps(int* fps);
const char* insight9_receive_get_hardware_type(void);
```

### 5.7 Camera parameter API

```c
int insight9_receive_set_active_camera(int cam_id);
int insight9_receive_get_active_camera(int *cam_id);
int insight9_receive_set_camera_params(const camera_params *params);
int insight9_receive_get_camera_params(camera_params *params);
int insight9_receive_set_camera_params_for(int cam_id, const camera_params *params);
int insight9_receive_get_camera_params_for(int cam_id, camera_params *params);
int insight9_receive_reset_camera_params(int cam_id);
void insight9_receive_print_camera_params(const camera_params *params);
```

- `set_active_camera()` controls which camera is targeted by the generic parameter APIs.
- `set_camera_params_for()` and `get_camera_params_for()` operate on a specific camera index.
- All settings are range-validated before being sent to the device.

### 5.8 Calibration API

```c
int insight9_receive_get_camera_calib(int cam_idx, camera_calib *calib);
void insight9_receive_print_camera_calib(const camera_calib *calib);
int insight9_receive_align_depth_to_rgb(const uint16_t *depth,
                                        int depth_w, int depth_h,
                                        const camera_calib *left_calib,
                                        const camera_calib *rgb_calib,
                                        uint16_t *aligned_out,
                                        int rgb_w, int rgb_h);
```

- `insight9_receive_get_camera_calib()` reads factory intrinsics/extrinsics.
- `insight9_receive_align_depth_to_rgb()` warps left-camera depth into the RGB image plane.

## 6. Usage Example

This Windows SDK includes `example.cpp`, which demonstrates:
- SDK initialization
- callback registration
- starting capture
- parameter read/write
- console Ctrl+C handling

A minimal usage example:

```c
#include "Insight9SDK.h"
#include <stdio.h>
#include <windows.h>

static volatile BOOL keep_running = TRUE;

BOOL WINAPI console_handler(DWORD ctrlType) {
    if (ctrlType == CTRL_C_EVENT || ctrlType == CTRL_BREAK_EVENT) {
        keep_running = FALSE;
        return TRUE;
    }
    return FALSE;
}

void image_cb(int cam_id, uint8_t *data, size_t size,
              int width, int height, unsigned int format,
              uint64_t timestamp, uint64_t right_timestamp,
              void *userdata) {
    printf("Image[%d] size=%zu %dx%d fmt=0x%08X ts=%llu\n",
           cam_id, size, width, height, format, (unsigned long long)timestamp);
}

int main(void) {
    SetConsoleCtrlHandler(console_handler, TRUE);

    if (insight9_receive_init_default() != 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "SDK init failed\n");
        return -1;
    }

    insight9_receive_register_image_callback(image_cb, NULL);

    if (insight9_receive_start() != 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "SDK start failed\n");
        insight9_receive_cleanup();
        return -1;
    }

    printf("SDK running, press Ctrl+C to stop...\n");
    while (keep_running) {
        Sleep(100);
    }

    insight9_receive_stop();
    insight9_receive_cleanup();
    return 0;
}
```

## 7. Important Notes

- Data pointers provided in callbacks may point to internal SDK buffers that are reused. Copy any data you need to keep.
- Callbacks are invoked from capture threads. Avoid long blocking operations inside callbacks.
- `example.exe` depends on OpenCV DLLs, FFmpeg DLLs, and `hidapi.dll`. These are copied automatically to `build\Release` when building with the provided CMake setup.
- If OpenCV is missing, run `download_opencv.bat` and rebuild.
- The SDK requires Windows access to the UVC and HID devices; run the app with sufficient privileges if devices cannot be opened.
- If `insight9_receive_init_default()` fails, call `insight9_receive_cleanup()` to release any partially initialized resources.

## 8. Parameter Range Reference

| Parameter | Min | Max | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| exposure_time (s) | 0.0 | 0.03 | Linear mapping |
| exposure_gain | 1.0 | 16.0 | Linear mapping |
| auto_exposure | 0 | 1 | Boolean |
| brightness | 0.0 | 127.0 | Linear mapping |
| contrast | 0.0 | 1.9 | Linear mapping |
| gamma_dark | 1.0 | 4.0 | Linear mapping |
| hue | 0.0 | 87.0 | Linear mapping |
| saturation | 0.0 | 1.999 | Linear mapping |
| sharpness | 1 | 255 | Linear mapping |
| auto_white_balance | 0 | 1 | Boolean |
| white_balance | 1.0 | 3.0 | Linear mapping |
| decimation | 1 | 255 | Linear mapping |
| hardware_model | 0 | 3 | MCU/hardware selector |

## 9. Frequently Asked Questions

Q: How do I build the SDK on Windows?
A: Run `build.bat` in `Windows-SDK`, or use CMake with `-G "Visual Studio 18 2026" -A x64`.

Q: Why does `example.exe` fail to start?
A: Confirm the required DLLs are present in `build\Release`:
- `opencv_world*.dll`
- `avcodec-*.dll`, `avformat-*.dll`, `avdevice-*.dll`, `avutil-*.dll`, `swscale-*.dll`, `swresample-*.dll`
- `hidapi.dll`

Q: How do I set camera parameters for a specific camera?
A: Use `insight9_receive_set_camera_params_for(cam_id, &params)` and `insight9_receive_get_camera_params_for(cam_id, &params)`.

Q: How do I obtain calibration data?
A: Call `insight9_receive_get_camera_calib()` and then `insight9_receive_print_camera_calib()` to print it.

Q: Can I change FPS at runtime?
A: Use `insight9_receive_set_camera_fps(cam_id, fps)` and restart the affected camera if needed.

